\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Substituting a different task's instruction, on a band where each head scores well and at a power the original cell did not have. Both heads lose accuracy, so command coverage does not eliminate the instruction dependence it was built to remove; it halves it. Paired McNemar over matched trial indices (same seed, same initial states, same renderer --- only the instruction differs), exact two-sided.}}{9}{table.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:swap}{{8}{9}{Substituting a different task's instruction, on a band where each head scores well and at a power the original cell did not have. Both heads lose accuracy, so command coverage does not eliminate the instruction dependence it was built to remove; it halves it. Paired McNemar over matched trial indices (same seed, same initial states, same renderer --- only the instruction differs), exact two-sided}{table.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {7.1}Randomisation is a curve, not a point}{9}{subsection.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:sweep}{{7.1}{9}{Randomisation is a curve, not a point}{subsection.7.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Layer 4 drawn rather than argued. The released head asked for three objects emits three place points, $14.5$ and $13.0$\,cm from the basket. The basket is \emph  {not} fixed --- it is re-placed every episode, with a placement radius of $1.83$\,cm averaged over the suite's ten tasks --- so a memorised place point is wrong by $14$\,cm against a target that moves by under $2$, and $14$\,cm is enough to release a grocery over open table. \textbf  {Only radial distance is measured}: the angles are chosen for legibility and carry no information, which is why no coordinate grid is drawn.}}{10}{figure.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:placepoint}{{6}{10}{Layer 4 drawn rather than argued. The released head asked for three objects emits three place points, $14.5$ and $13.0$\,cm from the basket. The basket is \emph {not} fixed --- it is re-placed every episode, with a placement radius of $1.83$\,cm averaged over the suite's ten tasks --- so a memorised place point is wrong by $14$\,cm against a target that moves by under $2$, and $14$\,cm is enough to release a grocery over open table. \textbf {Only radial distance is measured}: the angles are chosen for legibility and carry no information, which is why no coordinate grid is drawn}{figure.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Sweeping the placement-randomisation radius on the same draws. Randomisation robustness is a curve, and at $n{=}10$ this curve is not even monotone --- the undisplaced cell sits \emph  {below} $\pm 2$\,cm, which cannot be a real benefit of displacement. Reporting any single radius as ``passes randomisation'' would quote a number this sample does not resolve, and we do not use this table to set any threshold elsewhere in the paper. $^\ddagger $Per-trial outcomes for the $\pm 2$, $\pm 6$ and $\pm 8$ rows are in \texttt  {results/pod\_cells.json}; the two marked rows were measured in an earlier session and their trial records are not in the repository.}}{10}{table.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sweep}{{9}{10}{Sweeping the placement-randomisation radius on the same draws. Randomisation robustness is a curve, and at $n{=}10$ this curve is not even monotone --- the undisplaced cell sits \emph {below} $\pm 2$\,cm, which cannot be a real benefit of displacement. Reporting any single radius as ``passes randomisation'' would quote a number this sample does not resolve, and we do not use this table to set any threshold elsewhere in the paper. $^\ddagger $Per-trial outcomes for the $\pm 2$, $\pm 6$ and $\pm 8$ rows are in \texttt {results/pod\_cells.json}; the two marked rows were measured in an earlier session and their trial records are not in the repository}{table.caption.41}{}}
